---
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
---

<StudyLayout>
  <h1>Air around rock worlds · question test</h1>
  <p>
    15 November 2026 · independent clean-context, read-only reviewer. This is a
    framing test, not a design-quality or gameplay assessment.
  </p>
  <p><a href="/lab/studies/rock-atmospheres-2026-11-15">Study</a></p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Metric</th>
        <th>Result</th>
        <th>Reason</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>One subject</td>
        <td>Pass</td>
        <td>
          The envelope's appearance and entry response can be observed together
          in one flight example. Mercury's surfaces and Venus's orbit/gravity
          are fixed reference conditions.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Grounded</td>
        <td>Pass with findings</td>
        <td>
          The authored materials, spherical mapping, spin, light and Venus
          dimensions exist. Their status as chosen Lab exemplars needs
          distinguishing from production adoption.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Consequence</td>
        <td>Pass</td>
        <td>
          The answer adds a readable envelope to existing rock worlds, making a
          close pass offer an entry choice.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>Findings and response</h2>
  <p>
    Mercury 02.19 contains all three materials, but the planet spec adopts the
    mineral surface for Practice Mercury, not all three as production types. The
    opening now explicitly identifies the three as Lab exemplars chosen for this
    cycle. This preserves the current design decision without implying a broader
    rollout.
  </p>
  <p>
    The borrowed Venus baseline has a visual atmosphere only. Its spec excludes
    atmospheric collision, drag and damage. The opening now states this, so the
    new hypothesis remains distinct from preserved mechanics. Corrections were
    not rerun through the reviewer.
  </p>
  <h2>Repository evidence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Mercury study 02.19 and RockSurface016.svelte: named materials, identical
      mapping, light and independent 31.3-second spin.
    </li>
    <li>
      docs/specs/game/world/planets/spec.md: adopted Mercury mineral material;
      Practice Venus dimensions and existing contact/gravity rules.
    </li>
    <li>
      packages/mode-a/src/lib/practice-venus.ts and simulation.ts: radius
      approximately 40.327526u, orbit, travel and mass wiring.
    </li>
    <li>
      docs/specs/game/controls/spec.md and simulation.ts: shared input and
      production flight simulation.
    </li>
    <li>
      Venus study 02.2 and 02.7: separate body/edge roles and whole-planet
      inspection. docs/design.md: pleasurable flight and convincing materials.
    </li>
    <li>
      Study registry: Mercury and Venus remain open but concern fixed reference
      facets; no other open study owns the atmosphere question.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Not checked</h2>
  <p>
    Rendered page, visual quality, keyboard behavior, viewport layout, actual
    atmospheric response, exact Wasp fitting, asset loading, automated tests and
    human gameplay judgment. Prohibited failed studies and their associated
    artifacts were not opened; only registry metadata was used for overlap.
  </p>
</StudyLayout>
<style>
th,
td {
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
  padding: var(--spacing-sm);
}
</style>
